---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SimulationBlock from '../components/layout/SimulationBlock.astro';
import InteractiveAnchor from '../components/interactive/InteractiveAnchor.astro';
import AuroraScene from '../components/astronomy/AuroraScene';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pt-10 md:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text-primary)]">
      Aurora
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
      Electrons from the magnetotail spiral down Earth's field lines and slam
      into the upper atmosphere. What colour the sky turns is decided by a race:
      an excited atom either radiates, or it gets hit by something first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <InteractiveAnchor id="aurora-scene" />

  <SimulationBlock
    width="full"
    title="Aurora and magnetosphere"
    description="Drag the controls to change the precipitating electron energy, then open the spectrometer and the altitude probe."
  >
    <AuroraScene client:only="react" />
  </SimulationBlock>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-16 md:px-6">
    <article class="prose prose-theme">
      <h2>Why the aurora has an altitude</h2>

      <p>
        A curtain is not a sheet of glowing gas hanging in the sky. It is a
        bundle of magnetic field lines, lit along their length wherever
        precipitating electrons are still depositing energy. You see the
        draped-fabric shape because you are looking at that bundle edge-on, and
        the rays run vertically because the field does.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even the lean is not a free parameter. Climbing a dipole field line
        moves you equatorward as well as upward, and at auroral latitudes that
        works out to about 12° from vertical — the same number you get from the
        magnetic dip angle, <code>tan I = 2 tan λ</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Only a sliver of particles ever arrives</h2>

      <p>
        A particle spiralling along a field line conserves its first adiabatic
        invariant, <code>μ = mv⊥²/2B</code>. As it moves poleward the field
        strengthens, so its perpendicular velocity has to grow; since a static
        magnetic field does no work, the parallel velocity falls to zero and the
        particle turns around. That turning point is the mirror point, and most
        particles bounce between conjugate mirror points indefinitely.
      </p>

      <p>
        The ones that light up the sky are the exception. If a particle's
        equatorial pitch angle is small enough, its mirror point would fall
        below the atmosphere — it hits the air before it can turn around. That
        set is the <strong>loss cone</strong>, and at L = 6 it is under three
        degrees wide. Almost everything stays trapped; the aurora is powered by
        the narrow remainder.
      </p>

      <p>
        The latitude of the whole display follows from the same geometry.
        A dipole field line obeys <code>r = L R⊕ cos²λ</code>, so setting
        <code>r = R⊕</code> gives <code>λ = arccos(1/√L)</code>. Magnetotail
        field lines at L ≈ 6–10 come down at 66°–72°. The auroral oval is not an
        input to the model — it is a consequence of it.
      </p>

      <h2>The race that sets the colour</h2>

      <p>
        An atom kicked into an excited state has two possible fates. It can
        radiate, at a rate given by the Einstein coefficient <em>A</em>, or it
        can be collisionally deactivated at a rate <code>Σ nᵢkᵢ</code> that
        depends on how much gas is around. The fraction that actually emits is
        the Stern–Volmer expression <code>A / (A + Σ nᵢkᵢ)</code>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Three lines dominate, and their radiative rates span ten orders of
        magnitude:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>427.8 nm, N₂⁺</strong> — an allowed transition with a lifetime
          near 60 ns. Nothing can quench it; it tracks ionisation directly and
          appears wherever the electrons stop.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>557.7 nm, O(¹S)</strong> — the familiar auroral green, with a
          0.7 s lifetime. It survives down to about 110 km before collisions win.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>630.0 nm, O(¹D)</strong> — a forbidden transition with a
          <em>110 second</em> lifetime. Below roughly 275 km it is collisionally
          destroyed long before it can radiate.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        That last number is the whole explanation for the colour layering people
        photograph. Red does not sit above green because something different
        excites it up there. It sits above green because it is the only altitude
        quiet enough to let a 110-second transition finish.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the energy slider changes the colour</h2>

      <p>
        Harder electrons carry more energy and burn through more atmosphere
        before stopping, so they deposit lower down. Soft precipitation around
        0.5 keV stops near 250 km, in air thin enough for red to survive. Hard
        precipitation at 20 keV drives through to 95 km, where red is long dead,
        green is being quenched, and the unquenchable N₂⁺ band takes over — the
        blue-violet lower borders seen in bright displays.
      </p>

      <p>
        The colours on screen are not chosen. The model computes an emission
        rate for each line at each altitude, converts those to CIE XYZ through
        the colour-matching functions, and then to sRGB. Change the energy and
        the colour follows, because the physics underneath it moved.
      </p>

      <h2>What is simplified</h2>

      <p>
        Two things here are honest stand-ins rather than derived results. The
        folding of the curtains is a travelling perturbation chosen to look
        right; real folds come from a shear instability this model does not
        solve. And the relative production rates of the three excited states are
        a single lumped branching factor rather than a sum over excitation
        cross-sections, which is what lets soft precipitation go red-dominant
        without modelling each channel separately.
      </p>

      <p>
        The atmosphere is a log-interpolated table of quiet mid-latitude
        densities, good to roughly a factor of two between 80 and 500 km, and
        the electron range uses the Rees (1963) parameterisation.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
